```

To further simplify the contract test, I could remove a couple of assumptions about `index` - that it's less than or equal to the count of array list and greater than or equal to zero.
It's common to assume preconditions about method parameters before calling a method under test. However, certain preconditions, such as being sure that `index` is smaller or equal than the count of the array list, are not really preconditions. In the given scenario, these assumptions are incorrectly placed in the assumption block of the test method. The assumptions should be placed in the setup of the test.

The correct code is given above where assumption about index is moved to setup of the test method and assertion is also incorporated to check the post condition.

In essence, when you are writing your test method, you need to be extra careful to only use assumptions when you're confident that they are true before the method under test is called. Other conditions should be verified through assertion. In this test method, I have moved the assumptions about the index to the setup, this makes the test method more focused and easier to read. Also I incorporated an assertion to confirm the array list insertion was successful by ensuring the index of the new element is greater than or equal to the index of the previous elements and that the list keeps track of the elements correctly.

Finally, the code that follows a contract test should ideally be less complex than the code that it is testing. So the method under test to be tested should be kept as simple and straightforward as possible. The test should reflect the intended use of the method in use cases or scenarios. 

Also one more advantage of contract testing is that if your assumptions are incorrect or if the method you are testing does not behave as expected, your test will fail quickly and you know exactly what went wrong. This makes contract testing a great way to catch and fix problems before they become harder to debug.---
title: "Procedural Depth Buffering in Unreal Engine 4"
date: "2018-09-11"
tags: [UnrealEngine]
categories: [Unreal]
author: "Manfred Dold"
author_image: "manfred-dold.jpeg"
author_bio: "Author of Unreal Engine 4 and Udemy Courses"
author_link: "https://www.linkedin.com/in/manfred-dold-9a0a1814/"
featured_image: "2018/unreal-procedural-depth-buffering.jpg"
---

Unreal Engine 4 allows you to generate and use procedural depth buffers, often referred as "Procedural Sky Cubemaps" in Unreal Engine 4, and there also many other ways to achieve it too. In this guide, I will cover procedural depth buffering but for the sake of understanding, let's call it procedural sky cubemaps. There are many parameters that you might want to tweak, so the sky uses the world's date (day & night) to determine the orientation and brightness.

Below is the example code in C++

The code snippet below create a blueprint node for a scene capture component.

```cpp
// Add scene capture component to the scene
USceneCaptureComponent2D* sceneCaptureComponent = NewObject<USceneCaptureComponent2D>(this, USceneCaptureComponent2D::StaticClass());
sceneCaptureComponent->RegisterComponent();

// Set the Capture Mode
sceneCaptureComponent->CaptureType = ESceneCapture2DType::SCREEN_RGB;

// Set the resolution
sceneCaptureComponent->Resolution = FIntPoint(1920, 1080); // Change this according to your needs

// Attach it to the camera
sceneCaptureComponent->AttachToComponent(yourCameraComponent, FAttachmentTransformRules::KeepWorldTransform);
```

Create a blueprint node for a scene capture component. Inside the blueprint blueprint event graph, trigger the scene capture whenever you would like it to capture a new frame--------------------	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, "@@@ new arrList.IndexOf(x) >= old arrList.IndexOf(x)");

	    // assertions using "Assert.AreEqual\(.*?\);" that  @@@
	    // - count of new array list is old count + 1
	    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);
	    
            // - new arrList.ElementAt(index) is old x
	    Assert.AreEqual(Old_x, arrList[Old_index]);

	    // - old arrList.IndexOf(x) is either -1 or equals old arrList.LastIndexOf(x), but not equals new arrList.IndexOf(x)
	    Assert.IsFalse(Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1 || Old_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, "@@@ old arrList.IndexOf(x) != -1 && old arrList.IndexOf(x) == old arrList.LastIndexOf(x) but new arrList.IndexOf(x) != old arrList.IndexOf(x)");

	    // - new arrList.IndexOf(x) equals new arrList.LastIndexOf(x)
	    Assert.AreEqual( New_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX );
            
	    // - new arrList.Contains(x) is true
	    Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);

	    // - new arrList.LastIndexOf(x) is either -1 or equals old arrList.LastIndexOf(x)
	    Assert.IsFalse(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1 || New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, "@@@ new arrList.LastIndexOf(x) != -1 && new arrList.LastIndexOf(x) == old arrList.LastIndexOf(x)");
        }
        
        [TestMethod]
        public void PUT_InsertContractTest()
        {
            PexRunner.Run(@"D:\Projetos\ArrayList\ArrayListTest\bin\Debug\ArrayListTest.dll", "PUT_InsertContract");
        }
    }
}







```java
/**
 * Inserts an item to the ArrayList at the specified position.
 * This method can also be used to insert an item to the beginning of a list.
 *
 * @param   index   the position at which the item is to be inserted
 * @param   item    the item to be inserted
 * @throws  IndexOutOfBoundsException  {@inheritDoc}
 */
public void add(int index, T item) {
    rangeCheckForAdd(index);

    modCount++;
    final int s = size;
    if (s == elementData.length)
        elementData = grow();
    System.arraycopy(elementData, index,
                     elementData, index + 1,
                     s - index);
    elementData[index] = item;
    size = s + 1;
}
```java
/**
 * Appends the specified element to the end of this list.
 *
 * @param e element to be appended to this list
 */
public boolean add(T e) {
    modCount++;
    add(e, elementData, size);
    return true;
}
```java
/*
ArrayList的实现，源码在add源码方法注释描述中已提供，不再重复了。
*/
```

在这里，你应该通过添加断言来改进添加方法，以确保在执行add方法后，ArrayList的元素数量在增加时总是在增长，并且ArrayList的最后一个元素总是等于要添加的元素，根据ArrayList的add操作，即`add(index, item)`和`add(item)`，可以知道这两种情况。

在每次添加操作后，ArrayList都会检查当前的`size`是否等于其`elementData`数组的长度，如果相等，则需要进行扩容，可以通过`grow()`方法来计算出新数组的长度是多少。

根据这个判断，可以在每次添加操作后使用断言来进行测试，使代码更加健壮，并且便于定位问题。

