### Test Case: TruncateAndCopy
The goal of this test case is to ensure that you have a correctly implemented `TruncateAndCopy` function in your ArrayList implementation. `TruncateAndCopy` function should copy the elements of the `ArrayList` from index 0 up to index `newLength`, and truncate the rest. If `newLength` is greater than the current length of the `ArrayList`, the function should not change the `ArrayList`. This function should preserve the order of the elements and not reverse it.

Below is the test code for this test case:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class ArrayList
{
    private object[] elements;

    public ArrayList(object[] elements)
    {
        this.elements = elements;
    }

    // Copy elements from index 0 up to newLength, truncate the rest
    public object[] TruncateAndCopy(int newLength)
    {
        if (newLength > elements.Length)
        {
            return elements;
        }

        object[] truncatedElements = new object[newLength];

        for (int i = 0; i < newLength; i++)
        {
            truncatedElements[i] = elements[i];
        }

        return truncatedElements;
    }
}

public class Program
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        ArrayList myList = new ArrayList(new object[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 });
        object[] truncatedElements = myList.TruncateAndCopy(4);
        
        // print the truncated list
        foreach (var element in truncatedElements)
        {
            Console.Write(element + " ");
        }

        // Output: 1 2 3 4 
    }
}
```

In the `Main` method, we first create an `ArrayList` with six elements. Then we call the `TruncateAndCopy` method on this `ArrayList` with a newLength of 4. The expected output is "1 2 3 4".

### Test Case: Clone
